🚀 𝗗𝗮𝘆 𝟭𝟱/𝟯𝟲𝟱 — 𝗗𝗲𝘃𝗢𝗽𝘀 𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗖𝗵𝗮𝗹𝗹𝗲𝗻𝗴𝗲

🔥 𝗞𝘂𝗯𝗲𝗿𝗻𝗲𝘁𝗲𝘀 𝗣𝗼𝗱 𝗶𝘀 𝗕𝗲𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗞𝗶𝗹𝗹𝗲𝗱 𝗪𝗶𝘁𝗵 𝗢𝗢𝗠𝗞𝗶𝗹𝗹𝗲𝗱 — 𝗪𝗵𝗮𝘁 𝗪𝗶𝗹𝗹 𝗬𝗼𝘂 𝗖𝗵𝗲𝗰𝗸?

𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗤𝘂𝗲𝘀𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻:

👉 “Your production pod is restarting frequently, and you notice the container status shows OOMKilled. How will you troubleshoot it?”

I wouldn't simply increase the memory limit immediately.

First, I would understand why the container is consuming more memory than expected.

🔍 𝗠𝘆 𝗧𝗿𝗼𝘂𝗯𝗹𝗲𝘀𝗵𝗼𝗼𝘁𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗙𝗹𝗼𝘄

1️⃣ Confirm the OOMKilled status

kubectl describe pod <pod-name> -n <namespace>

Look for:

Reason: OOMKilled

2️⃣ Check current resource usage

kubectl top pod <pod-name> -n <namespace>

Compare actual usage with the configured memory limit.

3️⃣ Check resource requests & limits

kubectl get pod <pod-name> -o yaml

Look for:

resources:
  requests:
    memory: "512Mi"
  limits:
    memory: "1Gi"

If the application exceeds its memory limit, Kubernetes can terminate the container.

4️⃣ Check application logs

kubectl logs <pod-name> --previous -n <namespace>

Look for:

🔹 Memory-related errors
🔹 Large requests
🔹 Memory leaks
🔹 Unexpected traffic
🔹 Recent application changes

5️⃣ Check whether usage is increasing over time

I would use monitoring metrics to determine whether memory usage is:

📈 Gradually increasing → possible memory leak
📈 Suddenly increasing → traffic/configuration/change
📊 Consistently near the limit → limit may be too low

6️⃣ Check recent changes

I would investigate recent:

🔹 Deployments
🔹 Configuration changes
🔹 Traffic increases
🔹 Application/library changes

🎯 𝗧𝗵𝗲 𝗙𝗹𝗼𝘄 𝗜 𝗙𝗼𝗹𝗹𝗼𝘄:

OOMKilled → Usage → Limits → Logs → Trend → Recent Changes → Root Cause

Only after understanding the cause would I decide whether to:

✅ Fix an application memory leak
✅ Optimize configuration
✅ Adjust resource limits
✅ Scale the application
✅ Work with the development team

𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗹𝗲𝗮𝗿𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗴:

Don't treat OOMKilled by simply increasing memory.
